    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran’s unemployment rate measures joblessness among people participating in the labor force, rather than among all adults. Participation, employment, working hours, and differences between population groups are essential to interpreting the headline.",
    "tags": ["Unemployment", "Labor force", "Employment", "Gender", "Labor statistics"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T12:56:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T12:56:17Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Unemployment and Labor Force Participation in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Read Iran’s unemployment figures alongside labor force participation, employment, and differences between women, men, and younger workers."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "understanding-the-denominator",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Who counts as unemployed?",
        "content": "In international labor statistics, unemployment is a specific status. It generally combines being without work, being available to work, and actively seeking employment. The labor force includes employed and unemployed people; people outside it are excluded from the unemployment-rate denominator. Someone who wants a job but has stopped searching may therefore disappear from the headline measure without finding employment. [s1]\n\nThis distinction is central to reading Iran’s figures. An unemployment rate describes difficulty finding work among labor force participants. A participation rate asks how much of the working-age population is economically active. An employment-to-population ratio asks how much is employed. Together, these measures reveal more than unemployment alone. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "post-pandemic-labor-market",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Recovery with a participation problem",
        "content": "The World Bank’s Spring 2024 monitor reported unemployment of 8.1 percent in 2023/24 alongside labor force participation of 41.3 percent. Employment had recovered beyond its pre-pandemic level, but participation remained weak. The report attributed part of the decline in unemployment to discouraged people leaving the active labor force. [s2]\n\nThat combination changes the interpretation of an apparently favorable headline. More employment can represent a recovery while withdrawal from job search simultaneously reduces measured unemployment. Assessing the balance requires looking at employment and participation together, rather than assuming every fall in the unemployment rate represents a transition into work. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "unequal-opportunities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different experiences within the national average",
        "content": "For 2023/24, the monitor reported unemployment of 6.6 percent among men and 15 percent among women. It also identified higher unemployment among young people and university graduates. These are rates within their respective labor forces, not shares of all men, women, or graduates. [s2]\n\nSectoral change adds another dimension. The monitor described agricultural job losses associated with drought and water shortages, alongside employment expansion in industry and services. A national total can therefore conceal substantial changes in the location and type of work. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "labor-status-diagram",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "How the main labor market measures fit together",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Working-age population\"] -->|\"Participates in labor market\"| B[\"Labor force\"]\n  A -->|\"Outside labor force\"| C[\"People outside the labor force\"]\n  B -->|\"Has employment\"| D[\"Employed people\"]\n  B -->|\"Without work, available and seeking\"| E[\"Unemployed people\"]\n  E -->|\"Numerator divided by labor force\"| F[\"Unemployment rate\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The unemployment rate uses the labor force as its denominator. People outside the labor force are excluded, even when they would like employment but do not meet the search or availability criteria.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "current-evidence-and-limits",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading a changing labor market",
        "content": "The October 2026 World Bank outlook describes renewed labor market deterioration. Its recent quarterly discussion should not be appended to an annual unemployment series as though the periods were interchangeable. [s3]\n\nEmployment itself also leaves questions unanswered. Hours, earnings, informality, and working conditions help distinguish having work from having adequate work. Statistical comparisons additionally require consistent age thresholds, survey coverage, and definitions. For Iran, a reconciled annual series with those details is still needed; this draft therefore provides no trend chart. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
