  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-4-1-4",
    "page_path": "/explore/industry/macroeconomics/economic-growth/",
    "name": "Economic Growth",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/industry/macroeconomics/",
    "entity_type": "indicator",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Economic growth measures changes in Iran’s real output, separating production from price increases. Its interpretation requires attention to oil and non-oil activity, population, employment, and revisions to national accounts.",
    "tags": ["Economic growth", "Real GDP", "Oil economy", "National accounts", "Sanctions", "Investment"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T12:56:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T12:56:17Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Economic Growth in Iran: Real Output and Its Limits | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s real GDP growth, the role of oil and external restrictions, and why output growth differs from household welfare."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "what-growth-measures",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "More production, rather than higher prices",
        "content": "Gross domestic product, or GDP, measures final goods and services produced within an economy. Economic growth usually means the percentage change in real GDP: output measured after adjusting for price changes. Nominal output can rise because prices increase, even when production does not. Real growth is therefore the appropriate starting point for asking whether Iran’s economy is producing more. [s1]\n\nGrowth is a rate, not the size of the economy. A slower positive rate still indicates expansion; a negative rate indicates contraction. Nor does aggregate growth establish that average living standards improved. GDP does not describe how income is distributed, and it omits important dimensions of welfare, including environmental costs and unpaid household activity. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-growth-pattern",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A recovery that needs a longer perspective",
        "content": "The World Bank’s publication overview describes Iran’s recovery after a prolonged period of negligible growth during 2011–2020. It identifies recovering consumption and more favorable oil-sector conditions among the forces behind the initial rebound following the pandemic shock. That history makes the starting point important: growth after a downturn does not by itself establish a return to an earlier development path. [s2]\n\nThe latest inspected outlook reports slower growth followed by an estimated contraction in the short annual series below. It also describes damage to productive infrastructure during conflict. These are the World Bank’s assessments; the estimated annual result remains subject to revision. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "annual-growth-chart",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran: real GDP growth at market prices",
          "dataset_id": "recent-real-gdp-growth",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["real-gdp-growth"],
          "x_axis_label": "Iranian reporting year, using the source’s Gregorian-span label",
          "y_axis_label": "Annual real GDP growth (%)",
          "y_axis_min": null,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "The series moves from expansion to slower expansion and then an estimated contraction. The final observation is a source estimate, and the following forecast year is excluded."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "sanctions-and-output",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "External restrictions and domestic transmission",
        "content": "Research by Mehdi Raissi and M. Hashem Pesaran investigates sanctions using an indicator constructed from newspaper coverage. Its abstract reports effects on exchange rates, inflation, and output growth, with currency depreciation preceding higher inflation and weaker output. It also reports adverse effects on employment and participation. These findings provide an analytical explanation for links between external restrictions and domestic economic activity. [s4]\n\nThey do not establish that sanctions explain every fluctuation. An estimated relationship depends on the study’s identification strategy and period. Its counterfactual results should not be treated as observed production, and this page does not insert them into the annual dataset. The inspected abstract supports the broad findings summarized here, rather than a detailed assessment of the model. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpreting-growth-responsibly",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What to read alongside growth",
        "content": "A useful assessment pairs real GDP with employment and distributional evidence. Positive growth may still be insufficient to create enough jobs, and changes in total output do not reveal who receives additional income. [s1]\n\nFor the annual comparison here, the reporting period and publication vintage are as important as the headline. A full historical account requires a longer, methodologically consistent series and reconciliation of revisions. Until that work is complete, the chart establishes the recent direction reported by one publication, rather than a complete account of Iran’s growth performance.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      }
    ],
